WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.467
Modern post-quantum planning should begin with vetted standards and mature implementations,

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:05.657
not custom cryptography.

00:00:05.657 --> 00:00:10.421
NIST's post-quantum standardization work gives organizations a practical reference point for algorithms

00:00:10.421 --> 00:00:13.200
intended to address major public-key use cases.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.360
This course is not official NIST training, and standards will continue to evolve,

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.139
but the main engineering message is stable:

00:00:21.139 --> 00:00:26.299
use reviewed algorithms through well-maintained libraries and protocols rather than inventing new designs.

00:00:26.299 --> 00:00:31.460
ML-KEM is the major key-encapsulation concept teams will see in PQC migration discussions.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.048
At a high level,

00:00:33.048 --> 00:00:37.811
it supports establishing shared secret material in a way intended to resist

00:00:37.811 --> 00:00:40.590
both classical and cryptographically relevant quantum attacks.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.956
It is not a drop-in answer for every protocol by itself.

00:00:44.956 --> 00:00:50.514
It still has to be integrated into TLS, VPNs, APIs, products, certificates where applicable,

00:00:50.514 --> 00:00:54.880
and operational workflows that expect certain sizes, timings, and negotiation behavior.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.041
ML-DSA and SLH-DSA are digital signature concepts used for different post-quantum signature needs.

00:01:00.041 --> 00:01:04.804
ML-DSA is a lattice-based signature family, while SLH-DSA is stateless and hash-based.

00:01:04.804 --> 00:01:09.171
Practitioners do not need heavy math to understand the planning impact.

00:01:09.171 --> 00:01:13.537
They need to know which systems need signatures, who verifies them,

00:01:13.537 --> 00:01:17.507
how long signatures must remain valid, how large artifacts become,

00:01:17.507 --> 00:01:21.476
and whether the surrounding infrastructure can handle the new formats.

00:01:21.476 --> 00:01:25.049
Algorithms also come with parameter sets and security categories.

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:29.813
Those choices are not decorative; they influence security level, performance, artifact size,

00:01:29.813 --> 00:01:30.606
and interoperability.

00:01:30.606 --> 00:01:33.782
A standards-aware migration still requires testing, monitoring, governance,

00:01:33.782 --> 00:01:34.973
and rollback planning.

00:01:34.973 --> 00:01:37.752
Mature adoption means following reputable implementation guidance,

00:01:37.752 --> 00:01:40.530
tracking library and vendor support, documenting decisions,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.500
and avoiding unsupported combinations that only work in a lab.
